EXT. FLOODED RICE PADDY DIKE - DAWN

A narrow earthen dike cuts across a drowned paddy, mist lying on the water like a sheet. A single banyan tree stands at the far end. Distant mortar thuds roll across the flat.

LT. HARGREAVE, thirties, a young officer with a map case, crouches at the dike's edge. SGT. "DOC" BIANCHI, forties, a medic with a red-cross satchel, crouches beside him. PVT. LIN TRAN, twenty, a scout, lies prone in the mud at the lead. CPL. OKAFOR, thirties, the radioman, hunkers behind them with the set on his back, the antenna bending as he twists to look behind.

HARGREAVE
Doc, keep it down.

DOC
I am down, Lieutenant. I'm lying in a rice paddy.

LIN
Contact, left.

Hargreave lifts his head, Doc grabs the satchel, Okafor drops the handset, and all four of them flatten as a burst of fire stitches the water twenty feet away and sends up silver fans.

HARGREAVE
Okafor, call it in.

OKAFOR
Sir, I can't call it in. They're listening.

HARGREAVE
Then talk in code.

DOC
What code?

OKAFOR
The cornflakes code.

HARGREAVE
OKAFOR, do not say cornflakes.

OKAFOR ^
Sir, cornflakes is the code!

DOC
Gentlemen, I would like to survive the morning.

LIN
They're moving.

Hargreave raises a hand. Lin crawls back, drags the radio, thumbs the key twice. Static pops. A voice crackles, faint.

HARGREAVE
Everyone on me. We go in three.

DOC
Three what?

HARGREAVE
Three, Doc.

They rise as one, bent double, and splash down the dike toward the banyan with the fog swallowing them, all four of them running.
